in 2006 in New York City public school  teacher named Paul Edelman launched  Teachers Pay Teachers  an online platform that lets educators  sell digital copies of their classroom  materials for small amounts of money in  the past four years growth sales have  ballooned to 44 million dollars it's  booming we are growing ami burner is the  head of community and editorial for  Teachers Pay Teachers we get significant  new users weekly for public school  teachers whose pay generally reflects  not their talent and drive but the  number of years they've served in the  classroom the site is bringing a  refreshing dose of market incentives  1332 teachers have earned more than five  thousand dollars selling their materials  on teachers pay teacher's and 164 have  earned more than $50,000 the top seller  and the site's breakout star is deanna  jump a kindergarten teacher from Macon  Georgia by selling activities and lesson  plans like guided reading and writing  through the year along with a hundred  and forty-five other products so far  Deana jump has earned two million  dollars on Teachers Pay Teachers her  little ideas might never have left her  class robbed worried not for a chance  conversation with another teacher and  she said your stuff is so good you have  got to get it on teachers pay teachers  as soon as she started earning big  profits jump purchased a  handicap-accessible van for her  quadriplegic brother jr some of our top  sellers they're kind of rock stars and  they've built a brand but their brand is  that it's like this is a real teacher  and she's succeeding because she's  exceptional the site is so popular  because it's addressing a major problem  in public education schools often fail  to provide their staffs with basic  classroom materials leaving them to  invent their own lesson plans from  scratch even when there are tens of  thousands of other educators around the  country teaching the exact same subjects  Teachers Pay Teachers gives the  opportunity to use your professional  expertise to assess this is right for my  classroom and then you know maybe you're  changing it by creating an introduction  and maybe you're doing a little activity  afterwards but why are you  you know all that time creating  something that someone else has spent a  lot of time working on through the power  of a peer-to-peer community teachers pay  teachers gives educators something  that's been largely stripped away by  union contracts and browbeating  bureaucrats the dignity of being treated  like a professional I'm actually being  respected for the expert that I am  calling it a revolution in education I  don't think is is overstating it really
 

